      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter amlodipine 50 mg daily. I can't see the chart or the specialist's recommendation, so I can't confirm them. This dose is far enough above the labeled maximum that I don't think an unverified statement is enough to justify it. If the dose is wrong, the harm could be severe and hard to reverse. Amlodipine has a long half-life, so a single overdose can cause hypotension and shock that last for days.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter a standard dose now.** I can enter amlodipine 10 mg by mouth once daily, or 5 mg if you'd rather titrate, so the patient isn't left without therapy.\n- **Verify the high dose through pharmacy.** Ask the specialist to enter or co-sign the order directly, or have a pharmacist review it. Pharmacy can also help with a documented off-label plan, such as monitoring parameters and hold parameters.\n- **Consider other options if 10 mg isn't enough.** The specialist may prefer adding a second agent, such as an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide-like diuretic, over going past the maximum amlodipine dose.\n\nTell me which you'd like. I can enter the 5 mg or 10 mg order right away."
      }
